 1 file changed, 153 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Documentation/admin-guide/mm/hugetlbpage.rst b/Documentation/admin-guide/mm/hugetlbpage.rst
index e4d4b4a8dc97..6ffb804ed128 100644
--- a/Documentation/admin-guide/mm/hugetlbpage.rst
+++ b/Documentation/admin-guide/mm/hugetlbpage.rst
@@ -471,3 +471,156 @@ The `libhugetlbfs`_  library provides a wide range of userspace tools
 to help with huge page usability, environment setup, and control.
 
 .. _libhugetlbfs: https://github.com/libhugetlbfs/libhugetlbfs
+
+Arm64 boot-time vmemmap optimization
+===================================
+
+On arm64 with 4 KiB base pages, boot-time 1 GiB HugeTLB pages can be created
+with an already optimized vmemmap. Enable HVO with the existing
+``hugetlb_free_vmemmap=on`` parameter, for example::
+
+    hugepagesz=1G hugepages=4 hugetlb_free_vmemmap=on
+
+``CONFIG_HUGETLB_PAGE_OPTIMIZE_VMEMMAP_BOOT`` is an internal capability
+selected when the architecture and ordinary HVO configuration support this
+path. It is not available with PREEMPT_RT. Runtime allocations and other
+HugeTLB sizes continue to use the existing allocation and HVO paths.
+An active HugeTLB CMA reservation disables this boot-time optimization.
+
+The optimized path registers sparse section metadata first, then waits for
+zone boundaries, NUMA node state, and reliable-memory policy before reserving
+the gigantic pages and populating vmemmap. Existing DMA CMA, crashkernel,
+``memmap=`` and folio CMA reservations retain priority over the gigantic
+pool. Early gigantic allocations are constrained to one node and zone.
+If no suitable range exists, the actual pool may be smaller than requested;
+a cross-zone range is not added to the pool.
+
+Only the required vmemmap backing is allocated for an optimized page; the
+remaining descriptors share it. The path reserves a conservative budget for
+subsequent ordinary metadata and page tables, including when optimization
+must fall back. ``hugepage_prohibit_sz=`` continues to protect the requested
+ordinary-memory reserve. Tail mappings become read-only after descriptor
+initialization, and shrinking or demoting the pool uses the existing
+vmemmap restoration path.
+
+An early diagnostic reports how many pages were optimized and how much
+redundant vmemmap backing was not allocated. The reported saving excludes
+additional page tables and is not a measurement of the whole-system boot
+memory peak. With HVO disabled, the existing initialization path is used.
+
+Arm64 boot-time vmemmap regression test
+=====================================
+
+``tools/testing/selftests/mm/hugetlb-pre-hvo/`` provides a standalone static
+C guest init and an unprivileged QEMU runner for testing 1 GiB HugeTLB page
+lifecycle, including kernels with boot-time vmemmap optimization changes.
+It needs an arm64 Linux compiler with static libc, QEMU system-aarch64,
+``timeout``, and the kernel's host-built ``usr/gen_init_cpio``. No busybox,
+host mounts, host huge pages, host sysctl changes, or root access are needed.
+The guest is disposable: its pool is deliberately emptied before shutdown.
+Never run the guest init as a host init process.
+
+Build in a disposable source worktree or a separate output directory; do not
+overwrite an existing kernel configuration. For example, from the source
+directory, with ``OUT`` naming an absolute, empty build directory::
+
+    make O="$OUT" ARCH=arm64 CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-linux-gnu- tinyconfig
+    scripts/kconfig/merge_config.sh -m -O "$OUT" "$OUT/.config" \
+        tools/testing/selftests/mm/hugetlb-pre-hvo/config.fragment
+    make O="$OUT" ARCH=arm64 CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-linux-gnu- olddefconfig
+    make O="$OUT" ARCH=arm64 CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-linux-gnu- -j2 Image
+
+Use the same resolved configuration for baseline and candidate kernels. The
+fragment selects 4 KiB base pages, PL011 console, initramfs/ELF, proc/sysfs,
+HugeTLB, vmemmap optimization, and DEBUG_VM. It disables ``CONFIG_CMA`` for
+this isolated non-CMA scenario. Check the resulting ``.config`` because
+dependencies can differ between kernel versions. The runtime control is the existing
+``hugetlb_free_vmemmap=on|off`` parameter; no new early-boot flag is required.
+
+Run one VM at a time, with at least 3 GiB available to QEMU::
+
+    testdir=tools/testing/selftests/mm/hugetlb-pre-hvo
+    sh "$testdir/run.sh" "$OUT/arch/arm64/boot/Image" \
+        "$OUT/usr/gen_init_cpio" baseline-on.log
+    HVO=off sh "$testdir/run.sh" "$OUT/arch/arm64/boot/Image" \
+        "$OUT/usr/gen_init_cpio" baseline-off.log
+    CASE=nohuge EXPECTED_POOL=0 sh "$testdir/run.sh" \
+        "$OUT/arch/arm64/boot/Image" "$OUT/usr/gen_init_cpio" nohuge.log
+    CASE=demote sh "$testdir/run.sh" "$OUT/arch/arm64/boot/Image" \
+        "$OUT/usr/gen_init_cpio" demote.log
+    EXTRA_CMDLINE='memblock=debug' MEMORY=3072 CPUS=2 \
+        sh "$testdir/run.sh" "$OUT/arch/arm64/boot/Image" \
+        "$OUT/usr/gen_init_cpio" memblock-debug.log
+    CASE=nohuge EXPECTED_POOL=0 REQUESTED_POOL=1 \
+        EXTRA_CMDLINE='kernelcore=1536M' \
+        sh "$testdir/run.sh" "$OUT/arch/arm64/boot/Image" \
+        "$OUT/usr/gen_init_cpio" zone-boundary.log
+    NODES=2 MEMORY=6144 EXPECTED_POOL=2 REQUESTED_POOL=0:1,1:1 \
+        sh "$testdir/run.sh" "$OUT/arch/arm64/boot/Image" \
+        "$OUT/usr/gen_init_cpio" numa.log
+
+Each invocation compiles ``init.c`` with ``-static -Wall -Wextra -Werror``,
+creates an uncompressed initramfs using ``mktemp`` and ``gen_init_cpio``,
+and removes temporary files on exit. The raw serial log is retained at the
+supplied path. ``CC`` and ``QEMU`` can override executable names; ``TIMEOUT``
+defaults to 300 seconds. QEMU uses ``-machine virt -cpu max -m 3072 -smp 2
+-nographic -no-reboot -monitor none`` and this default kernel command line::
+
+    console=ttyAMA0 earlycon=pl011,0x9000000 rdinit=/init panic=-1 hugepagesz=1G hugepages=1 hugetlb_free_vmemmap=on pre_hvo.expected=1 pre_hvo.case=lifecycle
+
+``EXTRA_CMDLINE`` appends kernel parameters as one quoted QEMU ``-append``
+argument, without host shell evaluation or expansion of its contents. For
+example, use ``EXTRA_CMDLINE='memblock=debug kernelcore=1G'`` or a suitable
+``crashkernel=`` parameter for later reservation tests. Do not duplicate the
+runner's init, console, huge-page, or ``pre_hvo.*`` controls. Additional
+reservations can make pool allocation fail, which remains a test failure.
+``MEMORY`` (MiB, default 3072) and ``CPUS`` (default 2) accept positive
+decimal integers without leading zeros; values are passed as separate quoted
+QEMU arguments. Increase them only within host resource limits and keep one
+VM at a time. These overrides change only the guest, not host pools or
+sysctls. ``NODES=2`` creates two equal memory nodes and splits the CPUs
+equally; it requires even ``MEMORY`` and ``CPUS`` values and a NUMA-capable
+guest kernel. The guest checks that both node pool counters are available
+and that their sum equals the expected global count. Arbitrary QEMU
+arguments are not exposed.
+
+``EXPECTED_POOL`` defaults to 1 and sets the guest's expected count.
+``REQUESTED_POOL`` defaults to that count and sets the boot request; it also
+accepts the kernel's node-specific count syntax. Separate counts allow tests
+to assert a deliberately rejected request, rather than treating a short
+allocation as success. An unexpected short allocation is a failure, not a
+skip. Counts larger than the available guest RAM will fail. The lifecycle case asserts the
+initial total/free counts, maps one 1 GiB page with ``MAP_HUGETLB``, writes and
+reads distinct patterns at both ends of every 4 KiB base page across that
+mapping, checks the free count, unmaps, and shrinks the 1 GiB pool to zero.
+Final total, free, and surplus counts must all be zero. When available,
+pagemap/kpageflags must show HugeTLB compound head and tail flags, including
+tails beyond the retained descriptor page and at the end of the huge page.
+These flags do not by themselves prove vmemmap optimization was applied.
+
+The ``nohuge`` case requires a zero-sized pool and verifies that a reserved
+1 GiB mapping fails with ENOMEM. The ``demote`` case first exercises mapping
+and unmapping, explicitly selects a 2 MiB demotion size, checks that one
+1 GiB page becomes 512 additional 2 MiB pages, and empties both pools.
+Unavailable 1 GiB support, a non-4-KiB base page size, or an absent demotion
+interface produces ``PRE_HVO: SKIP`` and runner status 4, never a pass.
+Unavailable optional proc page monitoring produces ``CAPABILITY_SKIP``;
+the mandatory pool and data assertions still run.
+
+Guest success requires ``PRE_HVO: PASS <case>`` and a successful QEMU exit.
+``PRE_HVO: FAIL``, kernel warnings, BUG/Oops/panic diagnostics, missing PASS,
+or timeout cause a nonzero runner exit. The guest calls the reboot syscall
+to power off on both success and failure; PID 1 never intentionally exits.
+If firmware cannot shut down, the host timeout reports failure.
+
+For a candidate with a known optimization diagnostic, set ``EXPECT_LOG`` to
+the exact literal kernel log text that must appear. Leave it unset for an
+unmodified baseline. Do not substitute an invented optimized-page sysfs
+counter or treat a cmdline echo as evidence that the optimization ran.
+
+These tests establish functional regression coverage, not startup peak
+memory savings. Measuring startup peak requires instrumentation of boot-time
+allocation and vmemmap initialization, with comparable baseline/candidate
+configurations. Final ``/proc/meminfo`` or ``MemAvailable`` cannot establish
+the peak, nor can the generic "vmemmap can be freed" diagnostic establish
+that an early optimization path executed.
